  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Whalleya microplaca is a small, inconspicuous fungal species in the Lopadostomataceae family, characterized by its diminutive fruiting bodies and microscopic structures. This rare fungus is found in New England and plays an ecological role in wood decomposition and nutrient cycling in forest ecosystems.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Microscopic fruiting bodies",
      "description": "Features extremely small, dark perithecia (flask-shaped structures) that require magnification to observe properly",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Spore characteristics",
      "description": "Produces distinctive ascospores that are visible only under high magnification, aiding in definitive identification",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Host substrate",
      "description": "Typically found on decaying hardwood substrates, particularly on decomposing bark and wood surfaces",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Whalleya microplaca inhabits moist, shaded forest environments in New England where it colonizes dead or dying hardwood, contributing to natural wood decomposition processes.",
